To implement the "House that Jack Built" nursery rhyme logic, I will replace the placeholder methods in `House.java` with the actual implementation.

The rhyme is cumulative. Each verse adds a new line at the beginning (after "This is the...") and keeps all previous lines. I will define the parts of the rhyme in arrays and build the verses programmatically.

1.  Define the subjects and their corresponding actions in reverse order (from the last item "house that Jack built" up to the first item "horse and the hound and the horn").
2.  Implement `verse(int verse)` to construct the specific verse by iterating through the parts up to the requested verse number.
3.  Implement `verses(int startVerse, int endVerse)` to concatenate multiple verses separated by newlines.
4.  Implement `sing()` to return all verses from 1 to 12.

Here is the updated file:

House.java
```java
class House {

    private static final String[] SUBJECTS = {
        "the house that Jack built",
        "the malt",
        "the rat",
        "the cat",
        "the dog",
        "the cow with the crumpled horn",
        "the maiden all forlorn",
        "the man all tattered and torn",
        "the priest all shaven and shorn",
        "the rooster that crowed in the morn",
        "the farmer sowing his corn",
        "the horse and the hound and the horn"
    };

    private static final String[] ACTIONS = {
        "",
        "that lay in ",
        "that ate ",
        "that killed ",
        "that worried ",
        "that tossed ",
        "that milked ",
        "that kissed ",
        "that married ",
        "that woke ",
        "that kept ",
        "that belonged to "
    };

    String verse(int verse) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("This is ");
        for (int i = 0; i < verse; i++) {
            sb.append(SUBJECTS[i]);
            if (i < verse - 1) {
                sb.append(ACTIONS[i + 1]);
            } else {
                sb.append(".");
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    String verses(int startVerse, int endVerse) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = startVerse; i <= endVerse; i++) {
            if (i > startVerse) {
                sb.append("\n\n");
            }
            sb.append(verse(i));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    String sing() {
        return verses(1, 12);
    }

}
```
