response:
To implement the domino chain logic, I will replace the placeholder `formChain` method in `Dominoes.java` with a backtracking algorithm. This algorithm attempts to build a valid chain by trying each unused domino in both orientations, ensuring that the current end of the chain matches the start of the next domino. It also ensures that the final chain is a loop (first number matches the last number).

Here are the steps for the implementation:
1.  Handle edge cases: empty list (return empty list) and single domino (must be a double to form a loop).
2.  Use a recursive helper method `backtrack` that takes the current chain, the remaining dominoes, and the current end value.
3.  In `backtrack`, if all dominoes are used, check if the chain forms a loop (start value == end value).
4.  Otherwise, iterate through the remaining dominoes. For each domino, try adding it to the chain if its first value matches the current end. Also try the reversed orientation if its second value matches the current end.
5.  Recurse with the updated chain and remaining dominoes.
6.  If a valid chain is found, return it. If not, backtrack.

Dominoes.java
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Dominoes {

    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes == null || inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        List<Domino> result = backtrack(new ArrayList<>(), new ArrayList<>(inputDominoes), -1);
        if (result == null) {
            throw new ChainNotFoundException();
        }
        return result;
    }

    private List<Domino> backtrack(List<Domino> currentChain, List<Domino> remaining, int currentEnd) {
        if (remaining.isEmpty()) {
            if (currentChain.isEmpty()) {
                return new ArrayList<>();
            }
            int start = currentChain.get(0).getLeft();
            if (start == currentEnd) {
                return new ArrayList<>(currentChain);
            }
            return null;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < remaining.size(); i++) {
            Domino d = remaining.get(i);
            List<Domino> newRemaining = new ArrayList<>(remaining);
            newRemaining.remove(i);

            // Try orientation: left matches currentEnd
            if (currentEnd == -1 || d.getLeft() == currentEnd) {
                currentChain.add(d);
                List<Domino> result = backtrack(currentChain, newRemaining, d.getRight());
                if (result != null) {
                    return result;
                }
                currentChain.remove(currentChain.size() - 1);
            }

            // Try orientation: right matches currentEnd (flip the domino)
            if (currentEnd == -1 || d.getRight() == currentEnd) {
                Domino flipped = new Domino(d.getRight(), d.getLeft());
                currentChain.add(flipped);
                List<Domino> result = backtrack(currentChain, newRemaining, d.getLeft());
                if (result != null) {
                    return result;
                }
                currentChain.remove(currentChain.size() - 1);
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```
